WEBVTT

00:00.450 --> 00:02.969
This is Part 4: install it.

00:02.919 --> 00:07.363
The light string has the thinnest wire on the job, and everything we do protects it.

00:08.330 --> 00:11.983
The lights come with thin twenty gauge wire, and we can't change it.

00:11.933 --> 00:17.165
Everything we run up to it has one job: keep that thin wire at three point two amps or less.

00:17.165 --> 00:19.954
A smaller gauge number means thicker wire.

00:19.954 --> 00:22.176
Fourteen gauge is thickest.

00:22.176 --> 00:25.267
It works, but use up stock, don't buy more.

00:25.267 --> 00:27.871
Sixteen gauge is our feeder wire.

00:27.871 --> 00:30.834
Eighteen gauge is fine for one circuit too.

00:30.834 --> 00:33.530
Thicker wire doesn't make the job safer.

00:33.530 --> 00:36.019
The thin twenty gauge is still the limit.

00:36.970 --> 00:42.510
Feeder wire to an injection: sixteen-two or eighteen-two, FT-4, stranded.

00:42.460 --> 00:45.284
Thirty feet max from the box to the injection.

00:45.284 --> 00:47.032
Going through a wall?

00:47.032 --> 00:52.066
The FT-4 needs an ETL, CSA or UL number printed on it.

00:52.066 --> 00:54.937
No number, and it doesn't go through a wall.

00:54.937 --> 00:56.626
No exceptions.

00:56.626 --> 00:59.439
And stranded copper only, never solid.

00:59.439 --> 01:02.518
Solid wire gets hard and snaps at the ends.

01:03.474 --> 01:05.079
Keep water out.

01:05.029 --> 01:08.755
Put dielectric grease on the O-ring before you push a plug together.

01:08.755 --> 01:11.603
The O-ring does the sealing, not the plastic.

01:11.603 --> 01:13.317
Hand-tight only.

01:13.317 --> 01:15.908
Too tight makes it hard to change later.

01:15.908 --> 01:19.103
And push-in wire connectors stay inside the box.

01:19.103 --> 01:20.885
They're not waterproof.

01:21.842 --> 01:24.129
Never plug in with the power on.

01:24.079 --> 01:27.297
Turn the power off at the box first, every time.

01:27.297 --> 01:32.285
On a run over thirty lights, plugging in live kills the chip in the light before the one you touched.

01:32.285 --> 01:35.457
You won't see it happen, but it comes back as a callout.

01:35.457 --> 01:36.927
Power off.

01:36.927 --> 01:38.246
Plug in.

01:38.246 --> 01:39.670
Power on.

01:39.670 --> 01:40.931
Test.

01:41.890 --> 01:48.078
Track comes in one metre, thirty-nine inch pieces: black, white, charcoal, wood grain and silver.

01:48.028 --> 01:50.262
Match the fascia when you can.

01:50.262 --> 01:53.271
U-shape, or hat track, is our standard.

01:53.271 --> 01:55.354
The lights press in from below.

01:55.354 --> 02:00.852
T-shape track folds flat and uses one-light strings, for hard rooflines and commercial jobs.

02:00.852 --> 02:08.086
The parts: an L-connector for a ninety degree corner, a straight connector to join two pieces, and an end cap to close the last piece.

02:09.042 --> 02:10.577
Cutting corners.

02:10.527 --> 02:14.925
Two perfect forty-five degree cuts are almost impossible, so we don't try.

02:14.925 --> 02:18.826
Notch the first piece, a small cut so the wires can go through.

02:18.826 --> 02:21.222
Cut the second piece at forty-five.

02:21.222 --> 02:24.428
Lay it over the top, and slide it until it fits.

02:24.428 --> 02:28.178
Dry-fit it, then screw it, and check the wires aren't pinched.

02:28.178 --> 02:30.307
File every cut and notch.

02:30.307 --> 02:33.108
Sharp edges cut wire and cause shorts.

02:34.058 --> 02:35.883
Now screw the track up.

02:35.833 --> 02:38.460
Clean the fascia with rubbing alcohol.

02:38.460 --> 02:41.793
Hold the track up and check the lights will face the right way.

02:41.793 --> 02:44.860
Pre-drill pilot holes, so nothing splits.

02:44.860 --> 02:49.143
Use stainless or zinc screws, at least one every three to four feet.

02:49.143 --> 02:51.562
Never drywall screws outside.

02:51.562 --> 02:52.904
They rust.

02:52.904 --> 02:55.358
Where it's windy, use every hole.

02:55.358 --> 02:59.189
Then press in the lights, and give each one a soft tug to check it's in.

03:00.154 --> 03:04.652
Bridge gaps at doors and gables with extension cables, in corrugated conduit.

03:04.602 --> 03:07.264
Use a Y-connector at corners and turns.

03:07.264 --> 03:12.623
Add a signal booster before any extension over ten feet, and at every jump to the second floor.

03:12.623 --> 03:16.338
And put an end cap on the last light of every run to keep water out.

03:17.298 --> 03:18.914
Now the wiring.

03:18.864 --> 03:21.167
One circuit, one breaker.

03:21.167 --> 03:25.554
Each group of fifty lights gets its own feeder, from its own four amp breaker.

03:25.554 --> 03:28.031
The controller gets its own breaker too.

03:28.031 --> 03:32.464
At each injection, cut the positive, so power can't flow into the next group.

03:32.464 --> 03:35.114
The data wire runs all the way through.

03:35.114 --> 03:36.561
Never cut it.

03:36.561 --> 03:40.681
If one group has a problem, one breaker trips, and the rest stay lit.

03:41.642 --> 03:43.304
Do it in this order.

03:43.254 --> 03:45.396
Plan it from the marked-up photo.

03:45.396 --> 03:49.829
Pull one feeder to each injection, never two injections on one wire.

03:49.829 --> 03:53.984
Put each feeder on its own four amp breaker, and the controller on its own.

03:53.984 --> 03:57.919
Cut the positive at every injection, and leave the data wire alone.

03:57.919 --> 03:59.759
Grease every connector.

03:59.759 --> 04:03.405
Then power on, and test on Pure White before you pack up.

04:04.354 --> 04:06.815
They can look almost the same in the box.

04:06.765 --> 04:08.061
They're not.

04:08.061 --> 04:12.332
This is what we're fixing: one fourteen-two for everything, and no breakers.

04:12.332 --> 04:14.867
A hundred eighty lights on one path.

04:14.867 --> 04:17.517
If one shorts, nothing shuts off.

04:17.517 --> 04:20.712
The wire just gets hotter, up to twenty-five amps.

04:21.666 --> 04:24.428
Open the box and ask three questions.

04:24.378 --> 04:27.410
Is there a breaker for every wire leaving the box?

04:27.410 --> 04:30.223
Does each wire go to one injection only?

04:30.223 --> 04:31.577
Follow it.

04:31.577 --> 04:33.672
If it splits, it's wrong.

04:33.672 --> 04:35.952
And is each breaker four amps?

04:35.952 --> 04:38.209
A tripping breaker is good news.

04:38.209 --> 04:40.258
It means the safety works.

04:40.258 --> 04:41.843
Fix the circuit.

04:41.843 --> 04:43.950
Never put in a bigger breaker.

04:43.950 --> 04:45.755
Count the lights first.

04:45.755 --> 04:48.440
It's almost always more than fifty-six.

04:49.394 --> 04:50.628
Recap.

04:50.578 --> 04:56.169
Sixteen-two FT-4 stranded feeder, thirty feet max. Grease every plug, hand-tight.

04:56.169 --> 04:58.460
Power off before you plug in.

04:58.460 --> 05:00.995
Track is screwed, never glued.

05:00.995 --> 05:03.761
One feeder, one four amp breaker.

05:03.761 --> 05:06.226
Cut the positive, never the data.

05:07.186 --> 05:08.872
That's Part 4.

05:08.822 --> 05:11.808
Now take the Part 4 quiz in your training portal.
